select seed_test($seed${"external_id":"original-reading-1","title":"IELTS Reading Test 1","is_full_mock":false,"is_published":true,"source_provenance":{"origin":"original","watermark":null,"shippable":true,"repaired_by":null},"sections":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s1","kind":"reading","ordinal":1,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"The Return of the Beaver","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"For four hundred years the European beaver was absent from British rivers. It had been hunted to extinction by the sixteenth century, valued for its dense waterproof fur, for its meat, and for castoreum, a secretion once used in medicines and perfumes. Its disappearance was so complete that by the Victorian period most people regarded the animal as exotic, something belonging to Canadian forests rather than to the streams of Devon or Perthshire. The rivers themselves changed in its absence, though nobody at the time thought to record how."},{"label":null,"text":"The first legal reintroduction took place in Scotland in 2009, when eleven beavers from Norway were released into Knapdale Forest under a five-year trial. The trial was watched closely, and not only by conservationists. Farmers whose fields bordered the release site were sceptical, and some were openly hostile. A beaver can fell a mature willow in a single night, and a family group can flood several hectares of grazing land by damming a drainage ditch. These are not imagined complaints. They are the ordinary consequences of allowing a large rodent to rebuild a landscape according to its own preferences."},{"label":null,"text":"What the trial also produced was a body of measurement. Researchers recorded water chemistry above and below the new dams, counted invertebrates, and mapped the ponds that formed behind them. The results were consistent and, to many, surprising. Water leaving a beaver pond carried substantially less sediment than water entering it, because the still water allowed particles to settle. Nitrogen and phosphorus concentrations fell for the same reason. A structure built by an animal with no interest in water quality was, in effect, filtering the river."},{"label":null,"text":"The flood findings attracted more attention still. A beaver dam is not a wall but a leaky obstruction, and its effect on a river in spate is to slow the peak rather than to stop the water. On a small Devon stream monitored for five years, the arrival of a beaver family reduced peak flows during heavy rain by around thirty per cent. For a village downstream, thirty per cent can be the difference between a wet garden and a flooded kitchen. Engineered flood defences that achieve the same reduction cost a great deal and must be maintained; the beavers maintained their own."},{"label":null,"text":"None of this makes the animal straightforward to live with. The same instinct that slows a flood will block a culvert, and a blocked culvert can put a road under water. Beavers do not distinguish between a stream that needs damming and a drainage channel that a farmer dug at considerable expense. Where their ponds spread onto productive land, the loss is real and falls on one landowner while the benefit, spread across a catchment, falls on everyone else. This asymmetry, rather than any dispute about ecology, is what has made the reintroduction politically difficult."},{"label":null,"text":"Practical answers exist. A device known as a pond leveller, a pipe run through a dam and screened at the upstream end, allows water to pass at a rate the beavers cannot detect and therefore do not attempt to block. Culverts can be fenced. Trees of particular value can be wrapped in wire mesh at the base. Each of these costs money, and the argument in Britain has largely been about who should pay it rather than about whether the animal belongs. In 2022 the beaver was given legal protection in England, which settled the question of whether it could be removed without a licence, and sharpened the question of who compensates the farmer."},{"label":null,"text":"Elsewhere the transition is further advanced. Bavaria has had beavers since the 1960s and now supports more than twenty thousand of them. It also employs a network of trained volunteers who install levellers, negotiate with landowners, and, where nothing else will serve, trap and relocate a family. The scheme costs the state a modest sum each year, and its architects argue that this is simply the price of the flood protection and water treatment the animals provide without charge. The comparison is not perfect, but the accounting is at least honest about both sides."},{"label":null,"text":"What the British experience has clarified is that a reintroduction is not an ecological event with social consequences but a social arrangement with ecological consequences. The beaver will do what beavers do. Whether that is experienced as restoration or as damage depends almost entirely on whether the institutions around the river have been prepared for it."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s1:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 1-6\nComplete the notes below.\nChoose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q1","ordinal":1,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"Beaver extinct in Britain by the ……… century","options":null,"correct_answer":"sixteenth","accepted_variants":["16th","the sixteenth"],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":0,"start":115,"end":124,"text":"sixteenth"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q2","ordinal":2,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"Hunted for fur, meat and ………, used in medicines and perfumes","options":null,"correct_answer":"castoreum","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":0,"start":193,"end":202,"text":"castoreum"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q3","ordinal":3,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"First legal reintroduction: 2009, at Knapdale Forest, using animals from ………","options":null,"correct_answer":"Norway","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":88,"end":94,"text":"Norway"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q4","ordinal":4,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"Water leaving a beaver pond carried less ……… than water entering it","options":null,"correct_answer":"sediment","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":2,"start":301,"end":309,"text":"sediment"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q5","ordinal":5,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"A pipe run through a dam is called a pond ………","options":null,"correct_answer":"leveller","accepted_variants":["leveler","pond leveller","pond leveler"],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q6","ordinal":6,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"In Bavaria, trained ……… install the devices and negotiate with landowners","options":null,"correct_answer":"volunteers","accepted_variants":["volunteer"],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":6,"start":175,"end":185,"text":"volunteers"}}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s1:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 7-13\nDo the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1?\nWrite TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, or NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.","shared_options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q7","ordinal":7,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"Victorian observers recorded the changes that took place in rivers after beavers disappeared.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q8","ordinal":8,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"Some farmers near the Knapdale site opposed the trial.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q9","ordinal":9,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"The complaints made by farmers about beavers are exaggerated.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q10","ordinal":10,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"Beaver dams removed more nitrogen from the water than phosphorus.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q11","ordinal":11,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"On the Devon stream, beavers cut peak flows during heavy rain by roughly a third.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q12","ordinal":12,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"Beavers can tell the difference between a natural stream and a drainage channel.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q13","ordinal":13,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"Bavaria's beaver programme is funded entirely by private donations.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":""},{"label":"FALSE","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s2","kind":"reading","ordinal":2,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"How Cities Cool Themselves","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"A city is warmer than the countryside around it, and has been since cities were first built of stone. The effect was described in London in 1818 by the chemist Luke Howard, who noticed that his thermometer read consistently higher inside the city than at a station a few miles outside it. He attributed the difference to the burning of coal. He was partly right, but the larger cause was the fabric of the city itself: brick, stone and later asphalt absorb heat through the day and release it slowly through the night, so that a city never fully cools before the sun rises again."},{"label":"B","text":"The modern term for this is the urban heat island, and its magnitude is larger than most residents assume. On a still, clear summer night, a large city may be eight degrees warmer than the farmland at its edge. The gap is greatest after dark, which is precisely when it matters most: a body that cannot shed heat overnight begins the next day already under strain. The excess deaths recorded during European heatwaves fall disproportionately on the upper floors of dense urban housing, where the night-time temperature barely falls at all."},{"label":"C","text":"Trees are the intervention most often proposed, and the evidence for them is strong but frequently misstated. A tree cools in two ways. It shades the surfaces beneath it, preventing them from absorbing heat in the first place, and it transpires, converting liquid water to vapour and taking energy from the air to do so. Of the two, shade is by far the larger effect at street level. This matters for policy, because a tree planted where it shades a wall or a pavement does far more than the same tree planted in the middle of a park, and planting targets counted in trees rather than in shaded square metres reward the wrong choice."},{"label":"D","text":"Cool roofs work on a different principle. A dark roof may reach seventy degrees on a summer afternoon; the same roof painted white will sit perhaps thirty degrees lower, because it reflects most of the sunlight that strikes it rather than absorbing it. The technique is cheap, requires no maintenance beyond repainting, and reduces the cooling load of the building below as well as the heat released into the street. Los Angeles has coated more than a hundred kilometres of road surface with a reflective seal on the same reasoning, though the results at street level have been mixed, since a surface that reflects heat upward can make a pedestrian standing on it less comfortable rather than more."},{"label":"E","text":"Water is the oldest method and the least reliable. Fountains, canals and misting systems cool the air immediately around them, and a Persian courtyard garden remains an efficient piece of climate engineering. But evaporative cooling fails exactly when it is needed most, in humid heat, because air that is already close to saturation cannot take up much more vapour. Cities in humid climates that have invested in water features as a heat strategy have generally been disappointed. In dry heat the same features work extremely well, which is why the technique originated where it did. The distinction matters because water features are expensive to run and highly visible, which makes them attractive to a city wanting to be seen to act. A misting system installed in a humid summer will cost a great deal, photograph well, and lower the temperature by almost nothing. Engineers have understood this since the first psychrometric charts were drawn, and it is still the most commonly repeated mistake in urban heat policy."},{"label":"F","text":"The most effective measures are the least visible. Ventilation corridors, streets and open spaces aligned with the prevailing summer wind, allow cooler air from outside to move through a city at night. Stuttgart has protected such corridors in its planning law since the 1930s, refusing permission for buildings that would block the flow of cold air down from the surrounding hills. No resident of the city can point to the intervention, and no photograph captures it. It is simply a set of buildings that were never built, which is a difficult thing for a mayor to celebrate and an easy thing for a later administration to give away."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s2:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 14-19\nReading Passage 2 has six sections, A-F.\nWhich section contains the following information?\nWrite the correct letter, A-F.","shared_options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q14","ordinal":14,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"a description of two distinct mechanisms by which a single measure works","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q15","ordinal":15,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"an early recorded observation of the phenomenon, with an incorrect explanation","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q16","ordinal":16,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"a measure whose benefit is impossible to photograph","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q17","ordinal":17,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"the reason a technique fails in one climate but succeeds in another","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q18","ordinal":18,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"a figure for the temperature difference between a city and its surroundings","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q19","ordinal":19,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"an example of a measure that may not help people at street level","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s2:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 20-23\nComplete the sentences below.\nChoose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q20","ordinal":20,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Luke Howard believed the temperature difference he measured was caused by the burning of ……….","options":null,"correct_answer":"coal","accepted_variants":["burning coal"],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":0,"start":336,"end":340,"text":"coal"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q21","ordinal":21,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"The urban heat island is at its largest after ……….","options":null,"correct_answer":"dark","accepted_variants":["nightfall","after dark"],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q22","ordinal":22,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Planting targets should be counted in shaded ……… rather than in numbers of trees.","options":null,"correct_answer":"square metres","accepted_variants":["square meters","sq metres","sq m"],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":2,"start":595,"end":608,"text":"square metres"}},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q23","ordinal":23,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Stuttgart has protected its ventilation corridors through its planning ……….","options":null,"correct_answer":"law","accepted_variants":["planning law","its planning law"],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":257,"end":260,"text":"law"}}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s2:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 24-26\nChoose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q24","ordinal":24,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"The writer says the urban heat island matters most at night because","options":[{"label":"A","text":"city residents are more likely to be indoors then."},{"label":"B","text":"a body that cannot cool overnight starts the next day already strained."},{"label":"C","text":"thermometers are more accurate after dark."},{"label":"D","text":"the countryside cools more slowly than the city."}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q25","ordinal":25,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What does the writer suggest about tree-planting policy?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should prioritise parks over streets."},{"label":"B","text":"It relies on transpiration more than on shade."},{"label":"C","text":"Counting trees rather than shade encourages poor decisions."},{"label":"D","text":"It has been shown to be less effective than cool roofs."}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q26","ordinal":26,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"The writer's main point about Stuttgart's ventilation corridors is that","options":[{"label":"A","text":"they were introduced more recently than other measures."},{"label":"B","text":"their invisibility makes them politically vulnerable."},{"label":"C","text":"they cost more than the alternatives."},{"label":"D","text":"they work only in cities surrounded by hills."}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s3","kind":"reading","ordinal":3,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"The Case Against Multitasking","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"The belief that a person can attend to two demanding things at once is remarkably durable, and remarkably unsupported. What the research shows is not that people multitask badly but that, for tasks requiring conscious attention, they do not multitask at all. They switch. Attention moves from one task to the other and back, and each move carries a cost in time and in accuracy. The cost is small enough to escape notice from the inside, which is why the belief survives, and large enough to matter in aggregate, which is why it should not."},{"label":null,"text":"The cost has a name. Psychologists call it the switch cost, and it has been measured in laboratories since the 1920s using tasks of deliberate simplicity: sort these cards by colour, now by suit, now by colour again. Alternating between the two rules is reliably slower than doing a run of each, even when the participant knows the sequence in advance and even after extensive practice. Practice reduces the cost. It has never been shown to eliminate it."},{"label":null,"text":"Two components make up the cost, and separating them explains a good deal. The first is the time taken to reconfigure, to load the rules of the new task into working memory. The second is interference from the task just abandoned, which does not vanish on command but decays. This second component is the reason that returning to a difficult piece of work after an interruption feels like starting again: some of it genuinely is starting again, because the mental state that made the work possible has partly dissipated and must be rebuilt."},{"label":null,"text":"The practical scale of this was estimated in a widely cited study of office workers, which found that a worker interrupted at a task took on average around twenty-three minutes to return to it. The figure is often quoted as though the whole period were lost, which it is not; the worker was doing other things, some of them useful. What the figure captures is the length of the detour, not the size of the waste. Even read carefully, though, it describes a working day in which sustained attention is the exception rather than the arrangement."},{"label":null,"text":"An obvious objection is that some people are simply better at this than others, and the objection turns out to be almost exactly backwards. When researchers at Stanford sorted participants by how much media multitasking they reported doing and then tested them, the heavy multitaskers performed worse on every measure of attentional control. They were more distracted by irrelevant information, slower to switch between tasks, and less able to hold the right items in memory. The finding does not establish that the habit caused the deficit; people who filter poorly may simply be drawn to environments that demand less filtering. But the confident self-assessment of the heavy multitaskers was not supported by anything measured."},{"label":null,"text":"There is one important qualification. Tasks that have been practised to the point of automaticity make almost no demand on conscious attention, and can genuinely be combined with tasks that do. An experienced driver on an empty motorway can hold a conversation. The same driver in unfamiliar traffic will stop talking, usually without noticing, because the driving has reclaimed the attention the conversation was borrowing. The distinction is not between important and unimportant tasks but between automatic and controlled ones, and it is a distinction people are poor at judging in their own case."},{"label":null,"text":"None of this argues for a working life of uninterrupted concentration, which no organisation has ever achieved and few would enjoy. It argues for treating attention as a resource with a price rather than a capacity that can be divided at no cost. The practical implications are modest and mostly unwelcome: fewer channels demanding immediate response, longer blocks protected from them, and an acceptance that the person who appears to be doing four things is doing one of them at a time and doing each of them slightly worse."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s3:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 27-32\nDo the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3?\nWrite YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer, NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer, or NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this.","shared_options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q27","ordinal":27,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"People are generally aware of the cost of switching between tasks.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q28","ordinal":28,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"Practice can remove the switch cost entirely.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q29","ordinal":29,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"The twenty-three minute figure represents time that is entirely wasted.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q30","ordinal":30,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"Card-sorting experiments are a better method than studies of office workers.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q31","ordinal":31,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"The Stanford study proved that media multitasking damages attention.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q32","ordinal":32,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"People find it hard to judge which of their own tasks are automatic.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":""},{"label":"NO","text":""},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":""}],"correct_answer":"YES","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s3:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 33-36\nChoose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q33","ordinal":33,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"According to the writer, the belief in multitasking persists because","options":[{"label":"A","text":"the research supporting it is widely published."},{"label":"B","text":"the cost of switching is too small to be noticed from the inside."},{"label":"C","text":"employers reward the appearance of busyness."},{"label":"D","text":"most tasks are automatic rather than controlled."}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q34","ordinal":34,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"The second component of the switch cost explains why","options":[{"label":"A","text":"practice reduces switching time."},{"label":"B","text":"simple tasks are used in laboratory studies."},{"label":"C","text":"resuming difficult work after an interruption feels like beginning again."},{"label":"D","text":"some workers are interrupted more often than others."}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q35","ordinal":35,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What does the writer say about the Stanford finding?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It has been contradicted by later work."},{"label":"B","text":"It applies only to people who work in offices."},{"label":"C","text":"It shows the direction of cause and effect clearly."},{"label":"D","text":"It leaves the direction of cause and effect unresolved."}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q36","ordinal":36,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"The driving example is used to illustrate","options":[{"label":"A","text":"the difference between automatic and controlled tasks."},{"label":"B","text":"the danger of conversation while driving."},{"label":"C","text":"the benefits of extensive practice."},{"label":"D","text":"the unreliability of self-report."}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:s3:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 37-40\nComplete the summary using the list of words, A-H, below.\nWrite the correct letter, A-H.\n\nFor tasks that need conscious attention, people do not divide their attention but engage in 37 ______ , which is slower than working in runs. Part of the delay comes from 38 ______ , as the rules of the new task are loaded into working memory. Heavy media multitaskers performed worst on attentional tests, so their confident 39 ______ was not borne out. The writer concludes that attention should be treated as 40 ______ rather than as something that can be split at no cost.","shared_options":[{"label":"A","text":"switching"},{"label":"B","text":"interference"},{"label":"C","text":"automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"self-assessment"},{"label":"E","text":"reconfiguration"},{"label":"F","text":"a resource with a price"},{"label":"G","text":"a capacity to be divided"},{"label":"H","text":"immediate response"}],"questions":[{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q37","ordinal":37,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"switching between tasks, which is slower than working in runs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"switching"},{"label":"B","text":"interference"},{"label":"C","text":"automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"self-assessment"},{"label":"E","text":"reconfiguration"},{"label":"F","text":"a resource with a price"},{"label":"G","text":"a capacity to be divided"},{"label":"H","text":"immediate response"}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q38","ordinal":38,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"loading the rules of the new task into working memory","options":[{"label":"A","text":"switching"},{"label":"B","text":"interference"},{"label":"C","text":"automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"self-assessment"},{"label":"E","text":"reconfiguration"},{"label":"F","text":"a resource with a price"},{"label":"G","text":"a capacity to be divided"},{"label":"H","text":"immediate response"}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q39","ordinal":39,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"the heavy multitaskers' confident judgement of their own ability","options":[{"label":"A","text":"switching"},{"label":"B","text":"interference"},{"label":"C","text":"automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"self-assessment"},{"label":"E","text":"reconfiguration"},{"label":"F","text":"a resource with a price"},{"label":"G","text":"a capacity to be divided"},{"label":"H","text":"immediate response"}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"original-reading-1:q40","ordinal":40,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"what attention should be treated as","options":[{"label":"A","text":"switching"},{"label":"B","text":"interference"},{"label":"C","text":"automaticity"},{"label":"D","text":"self-assessment"},{"label":"E","text":"reconfiguration"},{"label":"F","text":"a resource with a price"},{"label":"G","text":"a capacity to be divided"},{"label":"H","text":"immediate response"}],"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]}]}$seed$::jsonb);

select seed_test($seed${"external_id":"reading-2","title":"IELTS Reading Test 3","is_full_mock":false,"is_published":true,"source_provenance":{"origin":"Reading (2).html","watermark":null,"shippable":false,"repaired_by":null},"sections":[{"external_id":"reading-2:s1","kind":"reading","ordinal":1,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"With rapid urbanisation, more people now live in cities than in the countryside, and this number is set to rise. For all the benefits cities bestow, they are expensive places. In some years, Tokyo records the highest cost of living; in others, Moscow. In 2014, for expatriates, Luanda, the capital of Angola, received the dubious accolade; and, for Angolans themselves, it had also suddenly become pricey with real estate going through the roof, so to speak, and food being prohibitive – even locally sourced mangoes were $5 a kilo."},{"label":null,"text":"What can urbanites do to reduce the financial burden of paying for food? Diet? Grow their own? Beg for subsidies? Some economists have proposed that they buy contracts giving rights to a food stream in perpetuity, for example, a kilogram of beef would be delivered weekly from the date the contract started until the end of the owner's life. In essence, this is what house purchase is – indefinite security of a single commodity. As is the case with buying a house, a loan from a financial institution might be necessary for the beef contract, even if it were merely for Australian blade steak and not Japanese Wagu. The contract could also be sold at the current market price if its owner moved out of delivery range or renounced beef."},{"label":null,"text":"In order to maintain or increase the value of their investment, it is likely some owners would support national and international policies to limit food production – a sound idea in a world where 40% of food goes to waste."},{"label":null,"text":"But let's imagine, in this system, a consumer purchased a 25-year contract for beef, which, over time, doubled in value. Naturally, at sale, the owner would make a tidy profit. Conversely, if mad cow disease erupted, and no one dared eat beef, then the vendor would suﬀer. If the owner had bought ten beef contracts, he or she might even go bankrupt in this scenario."},{"label":null,"text":"Let's also imagine that people bought contracts on items they had no intention of consuming: that the health-conscious purchased, yet eschewed, saturated-fat meat; that shrewd amoral vegetarians speculated in beef, as they already buy share portfolios in which multinational agri-business is represented, or they deposit money into banks that do just that on their behalf."},{"label":null,"text":"It is quite plausible that this speculative behaviour could lead to the overheating of the food-stream market. The state may intervene, attempting to cool things down, or it may tolerate such activity. Indeed, a government that proposed a capital gains tax or high death duties on food-stream contracts might be voted out in favour of another that believed in laissez-faire.* Besides which, an investment contract may be a way to realise wealth when there are few other possibilities either because the stock market is highly volatile, or much of the local economy generates little revenue, as is the case in Angola and many developing countries. Indeed, food-stream speculation could become a middle-class prerogative, indulged in by legislative members themselves."},{"label":null,"text":"I hope by now, you've realised this essay is a spoof. Yet, the fantastic food-stream market is reminiscent of the global housing market, where homeownership and property speculation have become the privilege of a few at great expense to the many, who either cannot participate, or sign their lives away to banks. You may also have realised that when I bring this topic up at a dinner party, for instance, I am usually shouted down, despite what I believe to be its inherent logic, because my friends consider a house as more tangible than a steak, and their identities are bound up with vague but powerful notions of property rights and independence."},{"label":null,"text":"I do concede that home-ownership oﬀers security (not having to move, being connected to one particular neighbourhood) and creativity (being able to modify and decorate as you please), but I would prefer people rent rather than buy in an eﬀort to lower property prices and to encourage investment in other sectors of the economy. Economists Moretti and Chang-Tai Hsieh of the University of Chicago have estimated that US output between 1999 and 2009 was 13% lower than it could have been because high housing costs forced so many people to move. Income locked up in housing could otherwise have been spent on local businesses, like restaurants or gyms, and job creation would likely have ensued."},{"label":null,"text":"So, next time you toss a steak on the barbecue, ponder whether we should treat food in the same way we treat housing, or whether we should treat housing as we do food."},{"label":null,"text":"*French for 'allow to do'. An economic doctrine advocating that commerce should be free of state controls of any kind."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-2:s1:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 1-7Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 1-7 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q1","ordinal":1,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Would guarantee access to one kind of food stream ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"in perpetuity","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":199,"end":212,"text":"in perpetuity"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q2","ordinal":2,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Due to its expense, a bank ____ might be needed to buy one","options":null,"correct_answer":"loan","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":468,"end":472,"text":"loan"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q3","ordinal":3,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Could be bought and sold at the current ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"market price","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":664,"end":676,"text":"market price"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q4","ordinal":4,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• When sold, could result in a decent ____ or a considerable loss","options":null,"correct_answer":"profit","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":3,"start":169,"end":175,"text":"profit"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q5","ordinal":5,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Could be purchased on food a person did not plan on ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"consuming","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":4,"start":82,"end":91,"text":"consuming"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q6","ordinal":6,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• May be one of the few legitimate ways to make money when the stock market is very ____ or other parts of the economy perform poorly","options":null,"correct_answer":"volatile","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":526,"end":534,"text":"volatile"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q7","ordinal":7,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Would probably be a privilege of the ____ and members of parliament","options":null,"correct_answer":"middle class","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s1:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 8-12Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 8-12 on your answer sheet write: TRUE if the statement agrees with the information FALSE if the statement contradicts the information NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q8","ordinal":8,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"8The writer makes an analogy between the current housing and food markets.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q9","ordinal":9,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"9The writer rents his or her own home.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q10","ordinal":10,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"10The writer's friends share his or her ideas on the property market.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q11","ordinal":11,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"11The writer thinks people like to own their homes because they can customise them.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q12","ordinal":12,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"12Because Americans spent so much on housing, other parts of the economy suﬀered.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s1:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Question 13What would be a suitable title for Passage 1? Choose the correct letter A-E. Write the correct letter in box 13 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q13","ordinal":13,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"","options":[{"label":"A","text":"How to make homes aﬀordable"},{"label":"B","text":"Buying a house is a bad investment"},{"label":"C","text":"Rethinking the housing market"},{"label":"D","text":"new model for buying and selling food"},{"label":"E","text":"The madness of house and food prices"}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s2","kind":"reading","ordinal":2,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"The Eﬀects of Deforestation","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"Every year it is estimated that roughly 5.2 million hectares (52,000 km2) of the forest is lost worldwide. That is a net figure, meaning it represents the area of the forest not replaced. To put this size in context, that is an area of land the size of Croatia lost every single year. There is a wide range of negative effects from deforestation that range from the smallest biological processes right up to the health of our planet as a whole. On a human level, millions of lives are affected every year by flooding and landslides that often result from deforestation."},{"label":"B","text":"There are 5 million people living in areas deemed at risk of flooding in England and Wales. Global warming, in part, worsened by deforestation, is responsible for higher rainfalls in Britain in recent decades. Although it can be argued that demand for cheap housing has meant more houses are being built in at-risk areas, the extent of the flooding is increasing. The presence of forests and trees along streams and rivers acts like a net. The trees catch and store water, but also hold the soil together, preventing erosion. By removing the trees, the land is more easily eroded increasing the risk of landslides and also, after precipitation, less water is intercepted when trees are absent and so more enters rivers, increasing the risk of flooding."},{"label":"C","text":"It is well documented that forests are essential to the atmospheric balance of our planet, and therefore our own wellbeing too. Scientists agree unequivocally that global warming is a real and serious threat to our planet. Deforestation releases 15% of all greenhouse gas emissions. One-third of the carbon dioxide emissions created by human activity comes from deforestation around the globe."},{"label":"D","text":"In his book Collapse, about the disappearance of various ancient civilisations, writer Jared Diamond theorises about the decline of the natives of Easter Island. European missionaries first arrived on the island in 1722. Research suggested that the island, whose population was in the region of two to three thousand at the time, had once been much higher at fifteen thousand people. This small native population survived on the island despite there being no trees at all. Archaeological digs uncovered evidence of trees once flourishing on the island. The uncontrolled deforestation not only led to the eradication of all such natural resources from the island but also greatly impacted the number of people the island could sustain. This underlines the importance of forest management, not only for useful building materials but also for food as well."},{"label":"E","text":"Forestry management is important to make sure that stocks are not depleted and that whatever is cut down is replaced. Without sustainable development of forests, the levels of deforestation are only going to worsen as the global population continues to rise, creating a higher demand for the products of forests. Just as important though is consumer awareness. Simple changes in consumer activity can make a huge difference. These changes in behaviour include, but are not limited to, recycling all recyclable material; buying recycled products and looking for the FSC sustainably sourced forest products logo on any wood or paper products."},{"label":"F","text":"Japan is often used as a model of exemplary forest management. During the Edo period between 1603 and 1868 drastic action was taken to reverse the country's serious exploitative deforestation problem. Whilst the solution was quite complex, one key aspect of its success was the encouragement of cooperation between villagers. This process of collaboration and re-education of the population saved Japan's forests. According to the World Bank, 68.5% of Japanese land area is covered by forest, making it one of the best performing economically developed nations in this regard."},{"label":"G","text":"There is, of course, a negative impact of Japan's forest management. There is still a high demand for wood products in the country, and the majority of these resources are simply imported from other, poorer nations. Indonesia is a prime example of a country that has lost large swaths of its forest cover due to foreign demand from countries like Japan. This is in addition to other issues such as poor domestic forest management, weaker laws and local corruption. Located around the Equator, Indonesia has an ideal climate for the rainforest. Sadly much of this natural resource is lost every year. Forest cover is now down to less than 51% from 65.4% in 1990. This alone is proof that more needs to be done globally to manage forests."},{"label":"H","text":"China is leading the way in recent years for replenishing their forests. The Chinese government began the Three-North Shelter Forest Program in 1978, with aims to complete the planting of a green wall, measuring 2,800 miles in length by its completion in 2050. Of course, this program is in many ways forced by nature itself; the expansion of the Gobi Desert threatened to destroy thousands of square miles of grassland annually through desertification. This is a process often exacerbated by deforestation in the first place, and so represents an attempt to buck the trend. Forested land in China rose from 17% to 22% from 1990 to 2015 making China one of the few developing nations to reverse the negative trend."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-2:s2:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 14-20The reading passage below has eight paragraphs, A-H. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. Write the correct number, i-xi, in boxes 14-20 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q14","ordinal":14,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"x","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q15","ordinal":15,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"i","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q16","ordinal":16,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"iii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q17","ordinal":17,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"v","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q18","ordinal":18,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"ii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q19","ordinal":19,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"xi","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q20","ordinal":20,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"},{"label":"x","text":"x"},{"label":"xi","text":"xi"}],"correct_answer":"viii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s2:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 21-26Complete the summary below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 21-26 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q21","ordinal":21,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"5 million","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":10,"end":19,"text":"5 million"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q22","ordinal":22,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"store","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":1,"start":460,"end":465,"text":"store"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q23","ordinal":23,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"landslides","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q24","ordinal":24,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"emissions","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q25","ordinal":25,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"management","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q26","ordinal":26,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"The effects of deforestation are widespread and various. Some examples include flooding at a local scale to the wider effects of global warming on a worldwide scale. In Britain, for example, ____ people live in areas at risk of flooding. This risk is increased by deforestation. Trees catch and ____ water lowering the chance of flooding. By removing trees land erosion is also higher, increasing the chance of ____ Deforestation also affects global warming by contributing 15% of the ____ of greenhouse gasses. To make sure that the cutting down of trees is done in a sustainable way, good forestry ____ is important. In most countries, more trees are cut down every year than planted. One country that is reversing this trend is China, making it one of the few nations to ____ . the more common negative trend.","options":null,"correct_answer":"reverse","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s3","kind":"reading","ordinal":3,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Film Noir","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"After the Second World War, a curious change came over the outlook of Hollywood films. Rather than the positive, happy-ending stories that dominated the silver screen before the war, pessimism and negativity had entered American cinema. This post-war disillusionment was evident in Hollywood and the movement became known as film noir."},{"label":null,"text":"One would be mistaken to call film noir a genre. Unlike westerns or romantic comedies, film noir cannot be defined by conventional uses of setting or conflict in a way that is common to genre films. Film noir is more of a movement, pinned to one specific point in time in much the same way as Soviet Montage or German Expressionism was. Instead, the defining quality of film noir was linked to tone, lighting and an often sombre mood."},{"label":null,"text":"True film noir refers to Hollywood films of the 1940s and early 1950s that dealt with dark themes such as crime and corruption. These films were essentially critiquing certain aspects of American society in a way film had never done before. Since that time there have occasionally been other great noir films made, such as Chinatown, but the mood and tone are often different to the original film noir movies. One possible reason for this is the time in which the films were made. A common perception of art is that it reflects the society and time in which it is made. That makes film noir of the Forties and Fifties quite inimitable because, luckily, the world has not had to endure a war of the scale and destruction of the Second World War again."},{"label":null,"text":"Paul Schrader, a writer of films like Taxi Driver and Raging Bull, sees film noir as one of Hollywood's best and least-known periods. In his essay Notes on Film Noir, he admits that classifying film noir is almost impossible because many films considered as film noir vary greatly in style. He observed that there were four main traditions in film noir. First were the films specifically about war and post-war disillusionment. Schrader believes these films were not only a reflection of the war but also a delayed reaction to the great economic depression of the 1930s. The trend in Hollywood throughout this period and into the war was to produce films aimed at keeping people's spirits up, hence the positivity. As soon as the war ended, crime fiction started to become popular, which mirrored growing disillusionment in America. Films such as The Blue Dahlia and Dead Reckoning picked up on a trend started during the war with The Maltese Falcon in 1941, which is seen as the first example of film noir."},{"label":null,"text":"Another film noir tradition was post-war realism. This style of the film was similar to some European films of the same era, such as Italy's neorealist films like Vittorio De Sica's Bicycle Thieves and Roberto Rossellini's Open City. Part of this style was created by filming in real locations and away from constructed sets. The honesty of this style of film suited the post-war mood in America and is demonstrated well in Jules Dassin's Night and the City, much of which was filmed in and around London."},{"label":null,"text":"The third tradition of film noir according to Paul Schrader involves what he characterises as 'The German Influence'. Especially during the 1920s German Expressionism was one of the most unique and creative forms of cinema. Many German, Austrian and Polish directors immigrated to America before or during the rise of Hitler and in part due to the increasing control and prevention of artistic freedom. Many of them, such as Fritz Lang and Billy Wilder, would find their way into the Hollywood system and to this day remain some of the most celebrated directors of all time."},{"label":null,"text":"It was the lighting developed in German Expressionism, in particular, that was most influential on film noir. The interplay of light and shadow created by chiaroscuro was highly suggestive of hidden darkness and was largely responsible for creating the mood and feeling of film noir. But it was the coupling of expressionist lighting with realistic settings that really gave film noir its authenticity. It is no surprise then that two of the most popular film noir feature films, Sunset Boulevard and Ace in the Hole, were both directed by Billy Wilder."},{"label":null,"text":"The final tradition of film noir noted by Schrader is what he dubs 'The Hard-Boiled Tradition'. He notes how American literature of the time was the driving force behind much of this style of film noir. Ernest Hemingway, Raymond Chandler and James M. Cain were tough, cynical and uncompromising and their work reflects this type of attitude. If German Expressionism influenced the visual aspect of film noir, it was this hard-boiled writing style that influenced the characters, stories and scripts depicted on screen. Raymond Chandler adapted the screenplay for the film noir classic Double Indemnity from a James M. Cain story. This writing team, with Billy Wilder, again directing, was the perfect combination for one of Hollywood's most celebrated films."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-2:s3:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 27-32Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in Reading Passage 3? In boxes 27-32 on your answer sheet, write YES if the statement agrees with the claims of the writer NO if the statement contradicts the claims of the writer NOT GIVEN if it is impossible to say what the writer thinks about this","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q27","ordinal":27,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"27 The First World War had a big influence on the types of films being made in Hollywood.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q28","ordinal":28,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"28 Film noir is an official genre.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q29","ordinal":29,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"29 True film noir can be from any time and be about any kind of social issue.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q30","ordinal":30,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"30 Filmmaker Paul Schrader believes that film noir is almost impossible to classify.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"YES","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q31","ordinal":31,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"31 Mixing light and shadow was mainly responsible for creating a unique mood and feeling of film noir.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"YES","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q32","ordinal":32,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"32 During the 1950s film noir was the most successful type of film at the box office.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s3:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 33-37Complete the notes below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 33-37 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q33","ordinal":33,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• A delayed ____ to the great economic depression.","options":null,"correct_answer":"reaction","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":3,"start":515,"end":523,"text":"reaction"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q34","ordinal":34,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Part of the style was created by shooting the films in real locations instead of on sets. Similar to European film styles such as ____ . in Italy.","options":null,"correct_answer":"neorealism","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q35","ordinal":35,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Many directors from Germany, Austria and Poland ____ to America during the 1920s and 1930s.","options":null,"correct_answer":"immigrated","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":267,"end":277,"text":"immigrated"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q36","ordinal":36,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Combining chiaroscuro lighting with filming in real locations gave film noir its ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"authenticity","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":6,"start":389,"end":401,"text":"authenticity"}},{"external_id":"reading-2:q37","ordinal":37,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• The hard-boiled writing style influenced the depiction of ____ , stories and scripts in film noir.","options":null,"correct_answer":"characters","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":7,"start":467,"end":477,"text":"characters"}}]},{"external_id":"reading-2:s3:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 38-40Complete each sentence with the correct ending, A-F Write your answers in boxes 38-40 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-2:q38","ordinal":38,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"38 After the war, instead of the positive films that existed in Hollywood before","options":[{"label":"A","text":"suited the mood in America well."},{"label":"B","text":"one of Hollywood's most notable films."},{"label":"C","text":"there were a lot more romantic comedies released in America."},{"label":"D","text":"was something most people were not ready for."},{"label":"E","text":"negativity had entered Hollywood films."},{"label":"F","text":"a film that very few people know about today."}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q39","ordinal":39,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"39 The honesty of post-war realism in film noir","options":[{"label":"A","text":"suited the mood in America well."},{"label":"B","text":"one of Hollywood's most notable films."},{"label":"C","text":"there were a lot more romantic comedies released in America."},{"label":"D","text":"was something most people were not ready for."},{"label":"E","text":"negativity had entered Hollywood films."},{"label":"F","text":"a film that very few people know about today."}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-2:q40","ordinal":40,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"40 Double Indemnity, directed by Billy Wilder, is","options":[{"label":"A","text":"suited the mood in America well."},{"label":"B","text":"one of Hollywood's most notable films."},{"label":"C","text":"there were a lot more romantic comedies released in America."},{"label":"D","text":"was something most people were not ready for."},{"label":"E","text":"negativity had entered Hollywood films."},{"label":"F","text":"a film that very few people know about today."}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]}]}$seed$::jsonb);

select seed_test($seed${"external_id":"reading-4","title":"IELTS Reading Test 5","is_full_mock":false,"is_published":true,"source_provenance":{"origin":"Reading (4).html","watermark":"@MINDLESS_WRITER","shippable":false,"repaired_by":null},"sections":[{"external_id":"reading-4:s1","kind":"reading","ordinal":1,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Andrew Carnegie: industrialist and philanthropist","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"Andrew Carnegie: industrialist and philanthropist"},{"label":null,"text":"The Scottish-born American industrialist and philanthropist Andrew Carnegie, who was the leader of the American steel industry from 1873 to 1901, donated large sums of his fortune to educational, cultural, and scientific institutions. He was born in 1835 in Scotland, the son of William Carnegie, a weaver, and Margaret Morrison Carnegie."},{"label":null,"text":"With the invention of weaving machines, William Carnegie's job disappeared, and eventually the family was forced into poverty. In 1848 the family left Scotland and settled in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, in the USA. William found a job in a cotton factory, but he soon quit to return to his home handloom, making linens and trying to sell them door-to-door. 1Andrew Carnegie also worked in the cotton factory(otasi bilan bir joyda), but after his father died in 1855, his strong desire to help take care of the family pushed him to educate himself. He became 2an avid reader, a theatergoer, and a lover of music(kitob/teatr/musiqa)."},{"label":null,"text":"Andrew Carnegie became a messenger boy in a telegraph office and later became a telegraph operator. Thomas A. Scott, superintendent of the western division of the Pennsylvania Railroad, made 3the eighteen-year-old his secretary(secretary). Carnegie was soon earning enough to buy a house for his mother. During the Civil War in the United States (1861-65), he helped organize the military telegraph system, but soon returned to Pittsburgh to take Scott's old job with the railroad."},{"label":null,"text":"Between 1865 and 1870 Carnegie made money through investments in several small iron mills and factories. He also traveled throughout England, selling the bonds of small United States railroads and bridge companies. 4Carnegie began to see that steel was eventually going to replace iron for the manufacture of rails, structural shapes, pipe, and wire(steel). In 1873 he organized a steel rail company and continued building by 5cutting prices(prices), driving out competitors, shaking off weak partners, and putting earnings back into the company. He never went public (sold shares of his company in order to raise money). Instead he obtained capital from profit, and when necessary 6from banks(banks), and he kept on growing, making heavy steel alone."},{"label":null,"text":"In the 1880s Carnegie's purchases included a majority stake in the H C Frick Company in Pennsylvania that had vast coal interests, and also the Homestead mills business. Frick became his partner and eventually chairman of the Carnegie Company. Carnegie had moved to New York City in 1867 to be close to the marketing centers for steel products, while Frick stayed in Pennsylvania as the general manager. They made a good team, Behind the scenes, 7Carnegie planned new projects and the improvement of existing plants(projects); Frick was the working director who watched over the mass-production programs that helped keep price down."},{"label":null,"text":"Carnegie spent his leisure time traveling. He also wrote several books, including Triumphant Democracy (1886), which pointed out what he thought were 8the advantages of life in the United States over the unequal societies of Britain and other European countries(TRUE). To Carnegie, access to education was the key to America's political stability and industrial accomplishments. In 1889 he published an article, 'Wealth', stating his belief that the rich had a duty to use their money to improve the welfare of the community. Carnegie remained a bachelor until his mother died in 1886. A year later he married Louise Whitfield and they had one child together. The couple began to spend six months each year in Scotland, though Carnegie kept an eye on business developments and problems."},{"label":null,"text":"Carnegie's absence from the United States was a factor in the Homestead mills strike of 1892. After acquiring Homestead, Carnegie had invested in new plants and equipment, increased production, and 9automated many of the mill's operations, cutting down the number of workers that were needed(TRUE). 10Carnegie believed that workers had a right to join unions and to bargain with management through their unions(FALSE — aksincha, ha huquqi bor deb hisoblagan). He also recognized the right to strike, as long as the action was conducted peacefully. He viewed strikes as trials of strength, with peaceful discussion resolving the conflict. In contract talks during 1892, Frick wanted to lower the minimum wage because of the need for fewer workers. The union representing his workers would not accept this and organized a strike."},{"label":null,"text":"Following a violent confrontation that resulted in the death of eight workers, soldiers were sent in to restore order and 11Carnegie was criticized for his lack of action(NOT GIVEN — Frickni ayblagani aytilmagan). In the 1890s Carnegie also began to meet with tougher competition from newer, bigger companies who were interested in controlled prices and sharing the market. Companies that he had sold to for years threatened to cut down their purchases unless be agreed to cooperate. These threats made him decide to fight back. 12He refused to enter into any agreements with other companies(FALSE — kelishmadi, aksincha). Moreover, he decided to invade their territories by making similar products and by expanding his sales activities into the west of the country. Eventually, though, he decided to sell his company to the newly formed U.S. Steel Corporation in 1901 for almost $500 million. Carnegie's personal share was $225 million."},{"label":null,"text":"In retirement, Carnegie began to set up trust funds 'for the improvement of mankind'. 13He built some three thousand public libraries all over the English-speaking world(FALSE — AQSHga cheklanmagan) and funded galleries, museums and concert halls in the US. He also built a group of technical schools, a research institute, and a foundation providing pensions for university professors. When Carnegie died in 1919, the continuation of his broad interests was put under the general charge of the Carnegie Corporation, with a donation of $125 million."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-4:s1:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Complete the notes below. Write ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q1","ordinal":1,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• found employment in the same workplace as his ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"father","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":2,"start":444,"end":450,"text":"father"}},{"external_id":"reading-4:q2","ordinal":2,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• educated himself through books, theatrical performances and ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"music","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":2,"start":603,"end":608,"text":"music"}},{"external_id":"reading-4:q3","ordinal":3,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• became ____ to Thomas A Scott at Pennsylvania Railroad","options":null,"correct_answer":"secretary","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q4","ordinal":4,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• realised that changing engineering needs would require a greater demand for ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"steel","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q5","ordinal":5,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"+ reducing ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"prices","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q6","ordinal":6,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"+ reinvesting profit and sometimes borrowing money from ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"banks","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q7","ordinal":7,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Carnegie's responsibilities: arranging future ____ and innovations for factories","options":null,"correct_answer":"projects","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s1:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the information given in the text? Choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q8","ordinal":8,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"8Carnegie believed that the USA offered more opportunities than European countries did.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q9","ordinal":9,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"9A smaller workforce was required at the Homestead factory after Carnegie took control.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q10","ordinal":10,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"10Carnegie prevented his workers from joining unions.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q11","ordinal":11,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"11Carnegie blamed Frick for the violent workers strike in 1892.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q12","ordinal":12,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"12In the 1890s Carnegie negotiated with his competitors.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q13","ordinal":13,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"13Carnegie's provision of funds for libraries was limited to those in the United States.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s2","kind":"reading","ordinal":2,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Creative Problem-Solving","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"Creative Problem-Solving"},{"label":null,"text":"Puzzle-solving is an ancient, universal practice, scholars say, and it depends on creative insight, or a primitive spark. Now, modern neuroscientists are beginning to tap its source."},{"label":"A","text":"In a recent study, researchers at Northwestern University in the United States found that people were more likely to solve word puzzles with sudden insight when they were amused, having just seen a short comedy routine. 'What we think is happening,' said Dr Mark Beeman, a neuroscientist who worked on the study, '23is that the humor, this positive mood, is lowering the brain's threshold for detecting weaker or more remote connections(Beeman),' which enable people to solve puzzles."},{"label":"B","text":"14This suggests that the appeal of puzzles goes far deeper than the pleasant rush of finding a solution. The very act of doing a puzzle typically shifts the brain into an open, playful state that is itself a pleasing escape(jarayondan zavqlanish). 17Unlike the social and professional mysteries in the real world, puzzles are reassuringly solvable; but like any serious problem, they require more than mere intellect to crack(taqqoslash)."},{"label":null,"text":"'It's imagination, it's inference, it's guessing; and much of it is happening subconsciously,' said Dr Marcel Danesi, a professor of anthropology at the University of Toronto, Canada. '20It's all about you, using your own mind, without any method or schema, to restore order from chaos,' Danesi said. 'And once you have, you can sit back and say, \"Hey, the rest of my life may be a disaster, but at least I have a solution\"(Danesi).'"},{"label":"C","text":"For almost a century scientists have used puzzles to study what they call 'insight thinking', the leaps of understanding that seem to come out of the blue. In one experiment, the German psychologist Karl Duncker presented people with a candle, a box of pins, and the task of attaching the candle to a wall. About a quarter of the subjects thought to use the pins to tack the box to the wall as a support—some immediately, and others after failed efforts to tack wax to the wall. 22According to Duncker, the creative leap seems to have been informed by subconscious cues(Duncker)."},{"label":null,"text":"In another well-known experiment, psychologists H.G. Birch and H.S. Rabinowitz challenged people to tie together two cords; the cords were hanging from the ceiling of a large room, too far apart to be grabbed at the same time. A small percentage of people solved it without any help, by tying something else to one cord and swinging it like a pendulum so that it could be caught while they held the other cord."},{"label":null,"text":"18In some experiments, researchers gave clues to those who were stumped—for instance, by bumping into one of the strings so that it swung(izohlar berildi). Many of those who then solved the problem said they had no recollection of the clue, though it very likely registered subconsciously."},{"label":"D","text":"All along, researchers have debated the definitions of insight and analysis, and some have concluded that the two are merely different sides of the same coin. 15Yet in an authoritative discussion of the research carried out so far, the psychologists Jonathan W. Schooler and Joseph Melcher concluded that the abilities most strongly correlated with insight problem-solving 'were not significantly correlated' with solving analytical problems(tadqiqotlar sharhi). Either way, creative problem-solving usually requires both analysis and insight."},{"label":null,"text":"Adam Anderson, a psychologist at the University of Toronto, Canada, argues that although when people are solving problems they may move back and forth between these abilities, 21they are truly different brain states(Anderson — \"different brain states\" aynan mos)."},{"label":"E","text":"At first, studies did little more than confirm that brain areas that register reward spiked in activity when people came up with a solution, that is to say, once they had completed a puzzle. However, in a series of recent studies, 19John Kounios, a psychologist at Drexel University in the United States, has imaged people's brains as they prepare to tackle a puzzle, but before they've seen it(savoldan oldingi faollik)."},{"label":null,"text":"Those whose brains show a particular signature of preparatory activity—one that is strongly correlated with 24positive moods(positive)—turn out to be more likely to solve the puzzles with sudden insight than with trial and error (the clues can be solved either way)."},{"label":null,"text":"Previous research has also found activation of cells in a certain area of the brain when people widen or narrow their attention—say, when they filter out distractions to focus on a difficult task, like concentrating on someone's 25voice(voice) in a noisy room. In the case of insight puzzle-solving, the brain seems to widen its attention, in effect making itself more susceptible to 26distraction(distraction)."},{"label":"F","text":"16In the humor study, Beeman had college students solve word-association puzzles after watching a short video showing a stand-up comedian. Beeman found that these students solved more of the puzzles overall, and significantly more by sudden insight, compared with when they'd seen a scary or boring video beforehand(qo'rqinchli/zerikarli video — kamroq yechilgan)."},{"label":null,"text":"This 'open' state of mind does not only apply to intellectual puzzles. In a study published last year, researchers at the University of Toronto found that people in positive moods picked up more background detail, even when they were told to block out distracting information during a computer task."},{"label":null,"text":"The findings fit with dozens of experiments linking positive moods to better creative problem-solving. 'The implication is that positive mood engages this broad, ... attentional state that is both perceptual and visual,' said Anderson. He explains that not only are people in a positive mood able to think more broadly, they are able to notice more visually."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-4:s2:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Reading Passage 2 has six sections, A–F. Which section contains the following information? NB You may use any letter more than once.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q14","ordinal":14,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"14 A claim that people enjoy the process of doing puzzles as well as finding the answers.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q15","ordinal":15,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"15 A review of studies that looked at the relationship between insight and analysis.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q16","ordinal":16,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"16 The finding that people were less likely to solve puzzles after viewing uninteresting or disturbing material.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q17","ordinal":17,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"17 A comparison between doing puzzles and dealing with life challenges.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q18","ordinal":18,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"18 A description of a study where the subjects were given hints by those conducting the research.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q19","ordinal":19,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"19 Details of a study in which the focus shifted to mental activity before a puzzle is attempted.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""},{"label":"F","text":""}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s2:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Look at the following statements (Questions 20–23) and the list of researchers below. Match each statement with the correct researcher, A–E.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q20","ordinal":20,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"20 Solving a puzzle may help people facing difficulties feel better.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q21","ordinal":21,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"21 Two distinctly separate functions of the brain are used when solving puzzles.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q22","ordinal":22,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"22 Some subjects were able to find a solution to the puzzle they were given without knowing how they had done it.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q23","ordinal":23,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"23 Seeing something funny helps people make links that may not be obvious at first.","options":[{"label":"A","text":""},{"label":"B","text":""},{"label":"C","text":""},{"label":"D","text":""},{"label":"E","text":""}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s2:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q24","ordinal":24,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Early studies showed that when people solved a puzzle, the parts of the brain linked to reward were more active. Studies by Kounios reveal that when people are feeling ____ during the preparatory stage, it is more probable that they will use insight to solve puzzles. The part of the brain that is affected is connected with the adjustment of people's attention. When someone is trying to listen to a ____ when the general sound level is high, the focus narrows. When people solve puzzles using insight, their focus becomes wider, and they are more open to ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"positive","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q25","ordinal":25,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Early studies showed that when people solved a puzzle, the parts of the brain linked to reward were more active. Studies by Kounios reveal that when people are feeling ____ during the preparatory stage, it is more probable that they will use insight to solve puzzles. The part of the brain that is affected is connected with the adjustment of people's attention. When someone is trying to listen to a ____ when the general sound level is high, the focus narrows. When people solve puzzles using insight, their focus becomes wider, and they are more open to ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"voice","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q26","ordinal":26,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Early studies showed that when people solved a puzzle, the parts of the brain linked to reward were more active. Studies by Kounios reveal that when people are feeling ____ during the preparatory stage, it is more probable that they will use insight to solve puzzles. The part of the brain that is affected is connected with the adjustment of people's attention. When someone is trying to listen to a ____ when the general sound level is high, the focus narrows. When people solve puzzles using insight, their focus becomes wider, and they are more open to ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"distraction","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s3","kind":"reading","ordinal":3,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"All in the family","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"All in the family"},{"label":null,"text":"A person's brothers and sisters can have a powerful effect on their adult behaviour"},{"label":null,"text":"We can choose our friends, the saying goes, but we cannot choose our family of origin. Our parents provide the genetic material and make powerful early role models, but 27even more influential in determining what kind of adult we will become are our brothers and sisters - our siblings(NO — ota-ona emas, aka-uka/opa-singil ko'proq ta'sirli). They occupy a position of unique intimacy in our lives and cast a longer shadow than many recognize or are prepared to acknowledge. By turns enraging and lovable, familiar and mysterious, 28our brothers and sisters are the human beings who people our first social relationships(NOT GIVEN — jinsi haqida gap yo'q). And 29a sibling relationship is the most enduring relationship many of us will ever have, less emotionally intense than the bonds we form with our spouses and our own children if only because they started when we were so young(NOT GIVEN — \"e'tibor kamayishi\" haqida emas)."},{"label":null,"text":"Surprising new research by Dr Gene Brody found that having older children who do well in school and are well liked by other children leads to parental 'basking' - increasing mothers' self-esteem. 30It is clear that in turn this is associated with more positive parenting of younger children, who display fewer behavioural problems as a result(YES). Conversely, parents who get a difficult first child may in turn experience a negative spiral of household tension."},{"label":null,"text":"International sports star Piri Weepu is a rugby hard man, but at least some of his tough temperament was forged long ago in the family home. His older brother Billy started rugby-tackling little Piri when he was just four - to 'harden him up'. It seemed to work: Piri followed his brother in playing for the under-sevens league before he even started school. There are as many such stories as there are families: in the formative hothouse of the family, siblings interact in complex ways with the powerful force fields of parents, genetics and personality already at work. 31It starts with birth order, which determines play roles: the firstborn leader, the middle-child mediator and the rebellious youngest. These are roles than can stick for life(B — kattaygandagi xarakterga ta'sir)."},{"label":null,"text":"Clinical psychologist Claire Cartwright says 32she has clients who believe, and it might not have been true, that they were treated differently from their siblings, that one child in the family was preferred over others(C — noaniq/xato bo'lishi mumkin). They are quite convinced that the parental judgments on them all those years ago were harsher, and that they were the ones who always got into trouble. Later, in the workplace, such a person might be particularly defensive, and so behave in a way more prone to attracting harsh judgements."},{"label":null,"text":"However, the research shows that being Mummy or Daddy's favourite has its own traps. If you were the easy-going, co-operative child at home, you probably excelled at pleasing parents and, later, bosses. But you might, as an adult, find it hard to be assertive with authority figures and lack direction. On the other hand, 33the sibling who is disruptive and annoying as a child may be able to reinterpret that role later, turning those attention-seeking characteristics into strengths like determination and leadership(C — salbiy xususiyat ijobiyga aylanadi)."},{"label":null,"text":"How is it that full siblings, despite sharing DNA, can turn out so differently? One answer is that, in fact, each sibling grows up in a different family, a unique micro-culture. For example, the firstborn is, for a while, an only child, and therefore has a completely different experience of the parents than those born later. 34The parents themselves are growing up too, weathering hardship or good fortune, so one sibling might experience stability and closeness while another might be raised in the midst of crisis(D — oila muhiti vaqt o'tishi bilan o'zgaradi)."},{"label":null,"text":"Of course, there are many positive aspects of sibling relationships. 35Annette Henderson, a lecturer in psychology, says firstborn children learn vocabulary more quickly than their siblings because they are not competing to spend one-on-one time with parents. But younger children in turn benefit from unintentional instruction from their bigger brothers and sisters, acquiring entire phrases and an understanding of social concepts such as politeness(G — learning experiences). Similarly, 36a Cambridge University study of 140 children found that even when there is a great deal of conflict, siblings will still create a rich world of play and make-believe that extends them developmentally(B — imaginative games). Love-hate relationships were common among the children, but even those who fought the most had as many positive interactions as the other sibling pairs. It is also true that children compete for parental attention by making themselves different from their brothers and sisters, particularly if they are close in age. 37A 2003 research paper studied adolescents from 185 families over two years, finding that those who changed, to differentiate themselves from their siblings, increased the amount of warmth they gained from parents, and also developed stronger personalities and a better sense of their own identity(F — greater individuality)."},{"label":null,"text":"Another consideration is that many families today are smaller and more intimate than their historical counterparts. 38This may be advantageous because siblings tend to know each other better and remain lifelong friends compared to children from bigger or more widely spaced families, who perhaps lose touch with one another after leaving home(I — closer relationships). Then there was a 2010 American study which found that 39having a sister, whether younger or older, meant that 10-to 14-year-olds were less likely to feel lonely, unloved, self-conscious or fearful(K — mental well-being). 40The same study found that having a loving sibling of either gender promoted good deeds, such as helping a neighbor around the house or helping other children at school(D — generous behaviour)."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"reading-4:s3:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Do the following statements agree with the views of the writer in Reading Passage 3? Choose YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q27","ordinal":27,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"27Parents and siblings play equal roles in shaping people's adult personalities.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NO","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q28","ordinal":28,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"28People's first social relationships tend to be with siblings of their own gender.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q29","ordinal":29,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"29People's relationships with their siblings tend to receive less attention when they marry.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q30","ordinal":30,"type":"yes_no_not_given","prompt":"30Parental 'basking' is advantageous for later siblings.","options":[{"label":"YES","text":"YES"},{"label":"NO","text":"NO"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"YES","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s3:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q31","ordinal":31,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What point does the writer make about birth order in the third paragraph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its role is underestimated by parents."},{"label":"B","text":"It may shape the characteristics of adults."},{"label":"C","text":"Its importance decreases as children grow up."},{"label":"D","text":"It has more influence on personality than genetics."}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q32","ordinal":32,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What are we told about some of Claire Cartwright's clients?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Their parents treated them fairly."},{"label":"B","text":"They were treated harshly by their siblings."},{"label":"C","text":"Their perceptions of childhood may be inaccurate."},{"label":"D","text":"They have closer relations with their siblings as adults."}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q33","ordinal":33,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"What is the writer's main point about siblings in the fifth paragraph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parents tend to repeat their own parents' mistakes."},{"label":"B","text":"Favored children are likely to make good leaders as adults."},{"label":"C","text":"Negative characteristics in children can become positive in adults."},{"label":"D","text":"Attention-seeking children are unlikely to please bosses in later life."}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q34","ordinal":34,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Which of the following best summarises the writer's argument in the sixth paragraph?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Many children have a favourite parent."},{"label":"B","text":"Parenting skills improve with later children."},{"label":"C","text":"Siblings are born with different personalities."},{"label":"D","text":"The family environment may change over time."}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"reading-4:s3:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Complete the summary using the list of words, A–K, below. Iborani sudrab bo'sh joyga tashlang.","shared_options":[{"label":"A","text":"parental love"},{"label":"B","text":"imaginative games"},{"label":"C","text":"physical development"},{"label":"D","text":"generous behaviour"},{"label":"E","text":"strong marriages"},{"label":"F","text":"greater individuality"},{"label":"G","text":"learning experiences"},{"label":"H","text":"career success"},{"label":"I","text":"I closer relationships"},{"label":"J","text":"J healthy competition"},{"label":"K","text":"K mental well-being"}],"questions":[{"external_id":"reading-4:q35","ordinal":35,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"G","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q36","ordinal":36,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q37","ordinal":37,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q38","ordinal":38,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"I","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q39","ordinal":39,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"K","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"reading-4:q40","ordinal":40,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"Annette Henderson suggests that young children's interactions with older siblings involve 35. Also, a Cambridge study found that siblings still enjoy 36 even when they have a tendency to fight. Research conducted in 2003 suggests that children develop 37 as a result of seeking attention. Another factor is the trend for smaller families, which may mean that today's siblings enjoy 38 as adults. A 2010 American study linked having a sister with 39 among children aged 10-14. Finally, having a sibling may promote 40 according to the same study.","options":null,"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]}]}$seed$::jsonb);

select seed_test($seed${"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1","title":"IELTS Reading Test 6","is_full_mock":false,"is_published":true,"source_provenance":{"origin":"Full CD Reading [M1] .html","watermark":null,"shippable":false,"repaired_by":"content/repairs/full-cd-reading-m1.json"},"sections":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s1","kind":"reading","ordinal":1,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Fishbourne Roman Palace","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"The largest Roman villa in Britain, Fishbourne Roman Palace is in the village of Fishbourne in West Sussex, England. This large palace was built in the 1st century AD, around thirty years after the Roman conquest of Britain on the site of Roman army grain stores which had been established after the invasion, in the reign of the Roman Emperor Claudius in 43 AD. The rectangular palace was built around formal gardens, the northern half of which have been reconstructed. There were extensive alterations in the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, with many of the original black and white mosaic floors being overlaid with more sophisticated coloured ones, including a perfectly preserved mosaic of a dolphin in the north wing. More alterations were in progress when the palace burnt down in around 270AD after which it was abandoned."},{"label":null,"text":"Local people had long believed that a Roman palace once existed in the area. However, it was not until 1960 that the archaeologist Barry Cunliffe, of Oxford University, first systematically excavated the site, after workmen had accidentally uncovered a wall while they were laying a water main. The Roman villa excavated by Cunliffe's team was so grand that it became known as Fishbourne Roman Palace, and a museum was erected to preserve some of the remains. It is administered by the Sussex Archaeological Society."},{"label":null,"text":"In its day, the completed palace would have comprised four large wings with colonnaded fronts. The north and east wings consisted of suites of private rooms built around courtyards, with a monumental entrance in the middle of the east wing. In the north-east corner there was an assembly hall. The west wing contained state rooms, a large ceremonial reception room, and a gallery. The south wing contained the owner's private apartments. The palace included as many as 50 mosaic floors, under-floor central heating and a bathhouse. In size, Fishbourne Palace would have been approximately equivalent to some of the great Roman palaces of Italy, and was by far the largest known Roman residence north of the European Alps, at about 500 feet (150m) square. A team of volunteers and professional archaeologists are involved in an ongoing archaeological excavation on the site of nearby, possibly military, buildings."},{"label":null,"text":"The first buildings to be erected on the site were constructed in the early part of the conquest in 43 AD. Later, two timber buildings were constructed, one with clay and mortar floors and plaster walls, which appears to have been a house of some comfort. These buildings were demolished in the 60s AD and replaced by a substantial stone house, which included colonnades, and a bath suite. It has been suggested that the palaces itself, incorporating the previous house in its south-east corner, was constructed around 73-75 AD. However, Dr Miles Russell, of Bournemouth University, reinterpreted the ground plan and the collection of objects found and has suggested that, given the extremely close parallels with the imperial palace of Domitian in Rome, its construction may more plausibly date to after 92 AD."},{"label":null,"text":"With regard to who lived in Fishbourne Palace, there are a number of theories; for example, one proposed by Professor Cunliffe is that, in its early phase, the palace was the residence of Tiberius Claudius Cogidubnus, a local chieftain who supported the Romans and who may have been installed as king of a number of territories following the first stage of the conquest. Cogidubnus is known from a reference to his loyalty in Agricola, a work by the Roman writer Tacitus, and from an inscription commemorating a temple dedicated to the gods Neptune and Minerva found in the nearby city of Chichester. Another theory is that it was built for Sallustius Lucullus, a Roman governor of Britain of the late 1st century, who may have been the son of the British prince Adminius. Two inscriptions recording the presence of Lucullus have been found in Chichester, and the redating by Miles Russell of the palace was designed for Lucullus, then it may have been only in use for a few years, as the Roman historian Suetonius records that Lucullus was executed by the Emperor Domitian in or shortly after 93 AD."},{"label":null,"text":"Additional theories suggest that either Verica, a British king of the Roman Empire in the years preceding the Claudian invasion, was owner of the palace, or Tiberius Claudius Catuarus, following the recent discovery of a gold ring belonging to him. The palace outlasted the original owner, whoever he was, and was extensively re-planned early in the 2nd century AD, and subdivided into a series of lesser apartments. Further redevelopment was begun in the late 3rd century AD, but these alterations were incomplete when the north wing was destroyed in a fire around 270 AD. The damage was too great repair, and the palace was abandoned and later dismantled."},{"label":null,"text":"A modern museum had been built by the Sussex Archaeological Society, incorporating most of the visible remains, including one wing of the palace. The gardens have been re-planted using authentic plants from the Roman period."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s1:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 1–6Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? In boxes 1–6 on your answer sheet, write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information FALSE if the statement contradicts the information NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q1","ordinal":1,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"1. Fishbourne Palace was the first structure to be built on its site.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q2","ordinal":2,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"2. Fishbourne Palace was renovated more than once.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q3","ordinal":3,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"3. Fishbourne Palace was large in comparison with Roman palaces in Italy.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q4","ordinal":4,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"4. Research is continuing in the area close to Fishbourne Palace.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q5","ordinal":5,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"5. Researchers agree on the identity of the person for whom Fishbourne Palace was constructed.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q6","ordinal":6,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"6. Fishbourne Palace was burnt down by local people.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s1:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 7–13Complete the sentences below.Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer.Write your answers in boxes 7–13 on your answer sheet. Fishbourne Palace","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q7","ordinal":7,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"7. The first buildings on the site contained food for the ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"Roman army","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":0,"start":239,"end":249,"text":"Roman army"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q8","ordinal":8,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"8. The palace building surrounded ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"gardens","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q9","ordinal":9,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"9. In the 2nd and 3rd centuries, colour was added to the ____ of the palace.","options":null,"correct_answer":"floors","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q10","ordinal":10,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"10. The first part of the palace to be found was part of a ____ .","options":null,"correct_answer":"wall","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q11","ordinal":11,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"11. Sallustius Lucullus may have lived there until approximately ____ AD.","options":null,"correct_answer":"93","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q12","ordinal":12,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"12. Catuarus – his ____ was found there.","options":null,"correct_answer":"gold ring","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":221,"end":230,"text":"gold ring"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q13","ordinal":13,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"13. A ____ has been built on the site to help protect it.","options":null,"correct_answer":"modern museum","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":6,"start":2,"end":15,"text":"modern museum"}}]}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s2","kind":"reading","ordinal":2,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Insect decision-making","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"It has long been held that decision made collectively by large groups of people are more likely to turn out to be accurate than decisions made by individuals. The idea goes back to the 'jury theorem' of Nicolas de Condorcet, an 18th-century French philosopher who was one of the first to apply mathematics to the social sciences. Condorcet's theory describes collective decisions, outlining how democratic decisions tend to outperform dictatorial ones. If, for example, each member of a jury has only partial information, the majority decision is more likely to be correct than a decision arrived at by a single juror. Moreover, the probability of a correct decision increases with the size of the jury."},{"label":"B","text":"Now it is becoming clear that group decisions are also extremely valuable for the success of social animals, such as ants bees, birds and dolphins. Bees make collective decisions, and they do it well, according to Christian List of the London School of Economics, who has studied group decision-making in humans and animals. Researchers led by Dr List looked at colonies once the original colony reaches a certain size. The queen goes off with about two-thirds of the worker bees to live in a new home or nest, leaving a daughter queen in the colony with the remaining workers. Among the bees that depart are some that have searched for and found some new nest sites, and reported back using a characteristic body movement known as a 'waggle dance' to indicate to the other bees the suitable places they have located. The longer the dance, the better the site. After a while, other bees start to visit the sites signaled by their companions to see for themselves and, on their return, also perform more waggle dances. The process eventually leads to a consensus on the best site and the breakaway swarm migrates. The decision is remarkably reliable, with the bees choosing the best site even when there are only small differences between alternative sites."},{"label":"C","text":"But exactly how do bees reach such a robust consensus? To find out, Dr List and his colleagues used a computer-generated model of the decision-making process. By experimenting with it they found that, when bees in the model were very good at finding nesting sites but did not share their information, this dramatically slowed down the migration, leaving the swarm homeless and vulnerable. Conversely, bees in the model blindly following the waggle dances of others without first checking. The researchers concluded that the ability of bees to identify successfully and quickly the best site depends on both the bees' interdependence in communicating the whereabouts of the bees site, and their independence in confirming this information for themselves."},{"label":"D","text":"Another situation in which collective decisions are taken occurs when animals are either isolated from crucial sources of information or dominated by other members of the group. José Halloy of the Free University of Brussels in Belgium used robotic cockroaches to subvert the behaviour of living cockroaches and control their decision-making process. In his experiment, the artificial bugs were introduced to the live ones and soon became sufficiently socially integrated that they were perceived by the real cockroaches as equals. By manipulating the robots, which were in the minority, Halloy was able to persuade the living cockroaches to choose an inappropriate shelter—even one which they had rejected before being infiltrated by the robots."},{"label":"E","text":"How quickly they accomplish the transfer to a new home depends not only on how soon the best available site is found, but also on how quickly the migration there can be achieved."},{"label":"F","text":"Once the suitable new nest is identified, the chosen ants begin to lead others, which have made it to the new site or which may simply be in the vicinity, back to the original threatened nest. In this way, those ants which are familiar with the route can help transport, for example, the queen and young ants to the new site, and simultaneously show the way to those ants which have been left behind to guard the old nest. In this way moving processes are accomplished faster and more efficiently. Thus the dynamics of collective decision-making are closely related to the efficient implementation of those decisions. How this might apply to choices that humans make is, as yet, unclear. But it does suggest, even for humans, the importance of recruiting dynamic leaders to a cause because the most important thing about collective decision-making, as shown by these insect experiments, is to get others to follow."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s2:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"READING PASSAGE 2You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 14–26, which are based on Reading Passage 2 on pages 7 and 8. Questions 14–19Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs A–F.Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below.Write the correct number, i–vii, in boxes 14–19 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":[{"label":"i","text":"The effect of man-made imitations on insects"},{"label":"ii","text":"The need to instruct additional insect guides"},{"label":"iii","text":"Signals used by certain insects to indicate a discovery"},{"label":"iv","text":"How urgency can affect the process of finding a new home"},{"label":"v","text":"The use of trained insects in testing scientific theories"},{"label":"vi","text":"The use of virtual scenarios in the study of insect behaviour"},{"label":"vii","text":"How the number of decision-makers affects the decision"}],"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q14","ordinal":14,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"14 Paragraph A ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"vii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q15","ordinal":15,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"15 Paragraph B ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"iii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q16","ordinal":16,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"16 Paragraph C ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"vi","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q17","ordinal":17,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"17 Paragraph D ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"i","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q18","ordinal":18,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"18 Paragraph E ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"iv","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q19","ordinal":19,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"19 Paragraph F ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"}],"correct_answer":"ii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s2:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 20–23Look at the following findings (Questions 20–23) and the list of academics below.Match each finding with the correct academic, A–D.Write the correct letter, A–D, in boxes 20–23 on your answer sheet. NB You may use any letter more than once.","shared_options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nicolas de Condorcet"},{"label":"B","text":"Christian List and colleagues"},{"label":"C","text":"José Halloy"},{"label":"D","text":"Nigel Franks and colleagues"}],"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q20","ordinal":20,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"20 Certain members can influence the rest of the group to alter a previous decision. ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q21","ordinal":21,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"21 Individual verification of a proposed choice is important for successful decision outcome. ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q22","ordinal":22,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"22 The more individuals taking part in a decision, the better the decision will be. ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q23","ordinal":23,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"23 The decision-making process of certain insects produces excellent results even when fine distinctions are required. ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s2:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 24–26Complete the summary below. Choose ONE WORD ONLY from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 24–26 on your answer sheet. A study of insect decision-making","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q24","ordinal":24,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"A Bristol University study looked at how insects make decisions when their home has been ____ . The ants in the experiment relied on the use of individuals called ____ to find a new nest and efficiently direct the others to go there. The study concluded that the effective implementation of the ants’ decision meant that the insects could change homes quickly. The study emphasized the necessity, for people as well as insects, of having active ____ in order to execute decisions successfully.","options":null,"correct_answer":"threatened","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":176,"end":186,"text":"threatened"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q25","ordinal":25,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"A Bristol University study looked at how insects make decisions when their home has been ____ . The ants in the experiment relied on the use of individuals called ____ to find a new nest and efficiently direct the others to go there. The study concluded that the effective implementation of the ants’ decision meant that the insects could change homes quickly. The study emphasized the necessity, for people as well as insects, of having active ____ in order to execute decisions successfully.","options":null,"correct_answer":"scouts","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q26","ordinal":26,"type":"summary_completion","prompt":"A Bristol University study looked at how insects make decisions when their home has been ____ . The ants in the experiment relied on the use of individuals called ____ to find a new nest and efficiently direct the others to go there. The study concluded that the effective implementation of the ants’ decision meant that the insects could change homes quickly. The study emphasized the necessity, for people as well as insects, of having active ____ in order to execute decisions successfully.","options":null,"correct_answer":"leaders","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":763,"end":770,"text":"leaders"}}]}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s3","kind":"reading","ordinal":3,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Fear of the Unknown","paragraphs":[{"label":"A","text":"American companies fear that innovation is the secret of success—and that they cannot innovate. In the small Umagic office in midtown Manhattan, a team of 30 computer programmers are working on setting up websites that will allow subscribers to feed in details about themselves and their problems and to receive advice from 'virtual' versions of personalities regarded as experts in their fields: for example, a well-known dietician, a celebrity fitness trainer, a psychologist well known in the media for her work on parent-child relationships. Umagic Systems is a young firm and it's hard to predict how far they'll go. In ten years' time, consulting a computer about your diet problems might seem natural or it might seem absurd. But the company and others like it are beginning to seriously worry large American firms, who see such half-crazy new and innovative ideas as a threat to their own future success."},{"label":"B","text":"Innovation has become a major concern of American management. Firms have found that it is increasingly difficult to redesign existing products or to produce them more economically. The stars of American business tend today to be innovators such as Amazon (the Internet bookstore) and Wal-Mart (the supermarket chain) which have produced entirely new ideas or products that have changed their industries."},{"label":"C","text":"Over the past 15 years, the firms which have achieved the greatest profits have been the ones which have had the most innovations. But such profits aren't easy to come by. One of the reasons for the increasing number of mergers between companies is a desperate search for new ideas. And a fortune is spent nowadays on identifying and protecting intellectual property: other people's ideas. According to the Pasadena-based Patent & License Exchange, the United States, trading in intangible assets such as intellectual property rose from $15 billion in 1990 to $100 billion in 1998, with an increasing proportion of the rewards going to small firms and individuals."},{"label":"D","text":"And therein lies the terror for big companies: that innovation seems to work best outside them. Many of the large established companies have been struggling to come up with new products recently. 'In the management of creativity size is your enemy,' argues Peter Chemin, who runs Fox TV and film empire for News Corporation. 'One person managing 20 movies is never going to be as involved as one doing five movies.' He has thus tried to break down the studio into smaller units, even at the risk of incurring higher costs."},{"label":"E","text":"It is easier for ideas to develop outside big firms these days. In the past, if a clever scientist had an idea he wanted to commercialise, he would take it first to a big company. Now, with the banks encouraging individuals to set up new businesses through offering special loans, innovators are more likely to set up on their own. Umagic has already raised $5 million and is about to raise $25 million more. Even in capital-intensive businesses such as pharmaceuticals, entrepreneurs can conduct profitable, early-stage research, selling out to the big firms when they reach expensive, risky clinical trials."},{"label":"F","text":"Some giants, including General Electric and Cisco, have been remarkably successful at buying up and integrating scores of small companies. But many others worry about the prices they have to pay and the difficulty in keeping hold of the people who dreamt up the ideas. 'Everybody would like to develop more ideas in-house,' says Frank and his associates reported how the insects reduce the problems associated with making a necessary swift choice. If the ants' existing nest become suddenly threatened, the insects choose certain ants to act as scouts to find a new nest."},{"label":"G","text":"Will all this reorganization and culture tweaking make big firms more creative? David Post, the founder of Umagic, isn't so sure: 'He also recalls with glee the looks of total incomprehension when he tried to sell his \"virtual experts\" idea three years ago to firms such as IBM, though, as he cheerfully adds, 'of course, they could have been right.' Apparently, innovation —unlike diet, fitness and parenting— is one area where a computer cannot tell you what to do."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s3:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 27–33Reading Passage 3 has eight paragraphs A–H.Which paragraph contains the following information?Write the correct letter, A–H, in boxes 27–33 on your answer sheet. NB You may use any letter more than once.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q27","ordinal":27,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"27 the methods some companies use to try to keep their most creative employees ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q28","ordinal":28,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"28 a new way of getting help with your personal difficulties ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q29","ordinal":29,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"29 how much investment goes into safeguarding the ideas of individuals ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q30","ordinal":30,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"30 two examples of companies which have succeeded through being innovative ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q31","ordinal":31,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"31 how some innovators manage to avoid spending large sums of money on testing out their ideas ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"E","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q32","ordinal":32,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"32 a commonly held opinion about product designers that needs to be proved wrong ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"G","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q33","ordinal":33,"type":"matching_information","prompt":"33 the target of one large company that has changed its business focus ____","options":[{"label":"A","text":"A"},{"label":"B","text":"B"},{"label":"C","text":"C"},{"label":"D","text":"D"},{"label":"E","text":"E"},{"label":"F","text":"F"},{"label":"G","text":"G"},{"label":"H","text":"H"}],"correct_answer":"F","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s3:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 34–37Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 3?In boxes 34–37 on your answer sheet, write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information FALSE if the statement contradicts the information NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q34","ordinal":34,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"34. Umagic Systems is an example of a new innovative company.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q35","ordinal":35,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"35. Amazon and Wal-Mart have exchanged successful ideas on innovation.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q36","ordinal":36,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"36. Using financial rewards to encourage innovation is an outdated practice.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q37","ordinal":37,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"37. IBM failed to understand David Post’s ‘virtual experts’ idea.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:s3:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 38–40Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.Write the correct letter in boxes 38–40 on your answer sheet.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q38","ordinal":38,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"38. What point does the writer make about intellectual property?","options":[{"label":"A","text":"It can be lost when firms merge."},{"label":"B","text":"It tends to belong to companies rather than individuals."},{"label":"C","text":"It is valued more than it used to be."},{"label":"D","text":"It is not usually owned by small companies."}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q39","ordinal":39,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"39. Peter Chemin is an example of someone who has realized that","options":[{"label":"A","text":"large companies are less innovative than small ones."},{"label":"B","text":"other businesses are more innovative than the film business."},{"label":"C","text":"his employees need more experience of innovation."},{"label":"D","text":"he is the best person to encourage innovation."}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-reading-m1:q40","ordinal":40,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"40. In conclusion, the writer suggests that","options":[{"label":"A","text":"computer-based industries cannot be innovative."},{"label":"B","text":"big firms are right to be cautious about innovation."},{"label":"C","text":"small firms should not worry about early failures."},{"label":"D","text":"innovation will always involve some uncertainty."}],"correct_answer":"D","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]}]}$seed$::jsonb);

select seed_test($seed${"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3","title":"IELTS Reading Test 8","is_full_mock":false,"is_published":true,"source_provenance":{"origin":"Full CD IELTS Reading Practice Test – 3_3.html","watermark":"@cd_ielts9","shippable":false,"repaired_by":null},"sections":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s1","kind":"reading","ordinal":1,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"The Pesticide-Free Village","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"Gerry Marten and Dona Glee Williams report on the Indian village of Punukula, so nearly destroyed by reliance on pesticides"},{"label":null,"text":"Around 20 years ago, a handful of families migrated from the Guntur district of Andhra Pradesh, south-east India, into Punukula, a community of around 900 people farming plots of between two and ten acres. The outsiders from Guntur brought cotton culture with them, and this attracted resident farmers by promising to bring in more hard cash than the mixed crops they were already growing to eat and sell, such as millet, mung beans, chilli and rice. But growing cotton meant using pesticides and fertilisers – until then a mystery to the mostly illiterate farmers of the community."},{"label":null,"text":"Local agrochemical dealers obligingly filled the need for information and supplies. These ‘middlemen’ sold commercial seeds, fertilisers and insecticides on credit, and guaranteed purchase of the crop. They offered technical advice provided by the companies that supplied their products. The farmers depend on the dealers. If they wanted to grow cotton – and they did – it seemed they had no choice."},{"label":null,"text":"A quick ‘high’ of booming yields and incomes hooked growers during the early years of cotton in the region. Outlay on insecticides was fairly low because cotton pests hadn’t moved in yet. Many farmers were so impressed with the chemicals that they started using them on their other crops as well. The immediate payoffs from chemically-dependent cotton agriculture both ensured and obscured the fact that the black dirt fields had gone into a freefall of environmental degradation, dragged down by a chain of cause and effect."},{"label":null,"text":"Soon cotton-eaters, such as bollworms and aphids, plagued the fields. Repeated spraying killed off the most susceptible pests and left the strongest to reproduce and pass on their resistance to generations of ever-hardier offspring. As the bugs grew tougher and more abundant, farmers applied a greater variety and quantity of poisons, something mixing ‘cocktails’ of as many as ten insecticides. At the same time, cotton was gobbling up the nutrients in the soil, leaving the growers no option but to invest in chemical fertilisers."},{"label":null,"text":"By the time some farmers tried to break free of their chemical dependence, insecticides had already decimated the birds, wasps, beetles, and other predators that had once provided natural control of crop pests. Without their balancing presence, pests ran riot if insecticide was cut back. As outlays for fertilisers and insecticides escalated, the cost of producing cotton mounted. Eventually the expense of chemical inputs outgrew the cash value of the crop, and farmers fell further and further into debt and poverty."},{"label":null,"text":"Their vicious cycle was only broken by the willingness of a prominent village elder to experiment with something different. He had been among the first villagers to grow cotton, and he would be the first to try it without chemicals, as set out by a programme in Non-Pesticide Management (NPM). This had been devised for Punukula with the help of a Non-Government Organisation called SECURE that had become aware of the hardships caused by the pesticide trap."},{"label":null,"text":"It involved turning to neem, a fast-growing, broad-leaved evergreen tree related to mahogany. Neem protects itself against insects by producing a multitude of natural pesticides that have evolved specifically to defeat plant-eating insects. Thus they are generally harmless to human and other animals, including birds and insects that eat pests."},{"label":null,"text":"The plant is native to India and Burma, where it has been used for centuries to control pests and to promote health. To protect cotton, neem seeds are simply ground into a powder, soaked overnight in water, and sprayed onto the crop at least every 10 days. Neem cake applied to the soil kills insect pests and doubles as an organic fertiliser high in nitrogen. As neem grows locally and is easy to process, it is much less expensive than the chemical insecticides sold for profit by the dealers and their corporate suppliers."},{"label":null,"text":"Quick, short-term gains had once pushed Punukula into chemical-dependent agriculture. Now they found that similar immediate rewards were helping to speed change in the other direction: the harvest of the next 20 NPM farmers was as good as the harvest of farmers using insecticides, and they came out ahead because they weren’t buying insecticides. Instead of investing cash (in short supply) in chemicals, they invested time and labour in NPM practices."},{"label":null,"text":"By the end of 2000, all the farmers in Punukula village were using NPM rather than chemicals for cotton, and they began to use it on other crops as well. The change gathered momentum as NPM became even more effective once everyone was using it. The status and economic opportunities of women improved – neem became a source of income for some of them, as they gathered seeds from the surrounding area to sell for NPM in other villages. The improve situation meant that families could afford to put more land under cultivation."},{"label":null,"text":"In 2004, the panchayat (village government) formally declared Punukula to be a pesticide-free village. And they have big plans for the future, such as water purification. The village now serves as a model for disseminating NPM to other communities, with around 2000 farmers visiting each year."},{"label":null,"text":"What began as a few farmers desperate to find a way to farm without poisons has become a movement with the potential to pull an entire region back from ecological disaster."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s1:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 1-4 Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 1? TRUE if the statement agrees with the information FALSE if the statement contradicts the information NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this Write your answers in boxes 1–4 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q1","ordinal":1,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"1Cotton growing was expected to raise more money than other crops.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q2","ordinal":2,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"2Some of the local agrochemical dealers had been farmers in the past.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q3","ordinal":3,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"3Initially the farmers’ cotton yields were low.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q4","ordinal":4,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"4At first, the farmers failed to notice the negative effects on their fields of pesticide use.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s1:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 5-10 Complete the notes below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 5–10 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q5","ordinal":5,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Based on use of an 5 ____ called neem","options":null,"correct_answer":"evergreen tree","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":7,"start":58,"end":72,"text":"evergreen tree"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q6","ordinal":6,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"• Neem contains many 6 ____ that target plant-eating predators","options":null,"correct_answer":"natural pesticides","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":7,"start":159,"end":177,"text":"natural pesticides"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q7","ordinal":7,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"○ 7 ____ formed by grinding seeds","options":null,"correct_answer":"a powder","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":8,"start":170,"end":178,"text":"a powder"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q8","ordinal":8,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"○ left 8 ____ to soak in water","options":null,"correct_answer":"overnight","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":8,"start":187,"end":196,"text":"overnight"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q9","ordinal":9,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"○ added in 9 ____ form to soil","options":null,"correct_answer":"neem cake","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":8,"start":257,"end":266,"text":"Neem cake"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q10","ordinal":10,"type":"note_completion","prompt":"○ contains a lot of 10 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"nitrogen","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":8,"start":351,"end":359,"text":"nitrogen"}}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s1:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 11-13 Answer the questions below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 11–13 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q11","ordinal":11,"type":"short_answer","prompt":"11 In which year did farmers finally stop using chemicals on cotton crops in Punukula? ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"2000","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q12","ordinal":12,"type":"short_answer","prompt":"12 What did the women of Punukula collect to make money? ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"neem cakes","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q13","ordinal":13,"type":"short_answer","prompt":"13 What project do the authorities in Punukula hope to set up in the future? ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"water purification","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":11,"start":151,"end":169,"text":"water purification"}}]}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s2","kind":"reading","ordinal":2,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"New Filter Promises Clean Water for Millions","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"An ingenious invention to bring clean water to developing countries"},{"label":null,"text":"An ingenious invention is set to bring clean water to developing countries, and while the science may be cutting edge, the materials are extremely down to earth."},{"label":null,"text":"A handful of clay, yesterday’s coffee grounds and some cow manure are the ingredients that could bring clean, safe drinking water to many developing countries. The simple new technology, developed by Australian National University (ANU) materials scientist and potter Tony Flynn, allows water filters to be made from commonly available materials and fired (or baked) using cow manure as the source of heat, without the need for a kiln (an oven for baking or drying pottery). The filters have been tested and shown to remove common pathogens (disease-producing organisms) including E-coli."},{"label":null,"text":"The invention was born out of a project involving the Manatuto community in East Timor. A charity operating there wanted to help set up a small industrial site manufacturing water filters, but initial research found the local clay to be too fine – a problem solved by the addition of organic material. While the problems of producing a working ceramic filter in East Timor were overcome, the solution was kiln-based and particular to that community’s materials and couldn’t be applied elsewhere. Flynn’s technique for manure firing, with no requirement for a kiln, has made this zero technology approach available anywhere it is needed."},{"label":null,"text":"Other commercial clay filters do exist, but, even if available, with prices starting at US$5 each, they are often outside the budgets of most people in the developing world. Unlike other water filtering devices, Flynn’s filters are inexpensive and simple to produce. Take a handful of clay, mix it with a handful of organic material such as used tea leaves, coffee grounds or rice hulls, add water in a sufficient quantity to make a stiff mixture and form a cylindrical pot that has one end closed, then dry it in the sun. According to Flynn, used coffee grounds have given the best results to date. The walls of the filter can be measure using the width of an adult finger as the standard. Next, surround the pots with straw, put them in a mound of cow manure, light the straw and then top up the burning manure as required. The filters are finished in 45 to 60 minutes."},{"label":null,"text":"The properties of cow manure are vital, as the fuel can reach a temperature of 700 degrees in half an hour, and will be up to 950 degrees after another 20 to 30 minutes. The manure makes a good fuel because it is very high in organic material that burns readily and quickly. The manure has to be dry and is best used exactly as found in the field; there is no need to break it up or process it any further. In contrast, a potter’s kiln is an expensive item and can take up to four or five hours to get up to 800 degrees. It needs expensive scarce fuel, such as gas or wood to heat it, and experience to use it. With no technology, no insulation and nothing other than a pile of cow manure and a match, none of these requirements apply."},{"label":null,"text":"It is also helpful that, like clay and organic material, cow manure is freely available across the developing world. A cow is a natural fuel factory. Manure is a mixture of vegetable materials of different sizes, and cow manure as a fuel is the same wherever it is found."},{"label":null,"text":"Just as using manure as a fuel for domestic use is not a new idea, the fact that liquid can pass through clay objects is something that potters have always known, and clay’s porous nature is something that, as a former ceramics lecturer in the ANU School of Art, Flynn is well aware of. The difference is that, rather than viewing the porous nature of the material as a problem – after all, not many people want a pot that won’t hold water – his filters capitalize on this property."},{"label":null,"text":"The filtration process is simple, but effective. The basic principle is that there are passages through the filter that are wide enough for water droplets to pass through, but too narrow for pathogens. Tests with the deadly E-coli bacterium have seen the filters remove 96.4 to 99.8 per cent of the pathogen – well within safe levels. The thickness of the clay container needs to be the same thickness as an adult finger for the process to be effective. If this is the case, using only one filter, a liter of water can be obtained in two hours."},{"label":null,"text":"The use of organic material, which burns away leaving cavities after firing, helps produce the structure in which pathogens will become trapped. It overcomes the potential problems of finer clays that may not let water through and also means that cracks are soon halted. And like clay and cow manure, organic material is universally available in the developing communities that need most assistance, as tea, coffee and rice are grown in these areas."},{"label":null,"text":"With all the components being widely available, Flynn says there is no reason the technology couldn’t be applied throughout the developing world. He has no plans to exploit his idea financially by registering ownership through a patent. If he did, any commercial copying would legally entitle him to a share in any profits made. Without a patent, there will be no illegality in it being adopted in any community that needs it. ‘Everyone has a right to clean water, and these filters have the potential to enable anyone in the world to drink water safely,’ says Flynn."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s2:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 14-19 Complete the flow-chart below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 14–19 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q14","ordinal":14,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Make the mixture for the filter from organic material (e.g. tea, coffee, rice), 14 ____ and 15 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"clay","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q15","ordinal":15,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Make the mixture for the filter from organic material (e.g. tea, coffee, rice), 14 ____ and 15 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"water","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q16","ordinal":16,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Shape into pots and place them in a fire made from 16 ____ and 17 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"cow manure","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q17","ordinal":17,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Shape into pots and place them in a fire made from 16 ____ and 17 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"straw","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q18","ordinal":18,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Fuel the fire to reach a maximum heat of 18 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"950 degrees","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":5,"start":126,"end":137,"text":"950 degrees"}},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q19","ordinal":19,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"Bake the filters in the fire for a maximum period of 19 ____","options":null,"correct_answer":"60 minutes","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":{"kind":"passage","paragraph_index":4,"start":860,"end":870,"text":"60 minutes"}}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s2:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 20-23 Do the following statements agree with the information given in Reading Passage 2? TRUE if the statement agrees with the information FALSE if the statement contradicts the information NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this Write your answers in boxes 20–23 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q20","ordinal":20,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"20The clay in the Manatuto project was initially unsuitable for the project’s purpose.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"TRUE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q21","ordinal":21,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"21Coffee grounds produce filters that are twice as efficient as those using other organic materials.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q22","ordinal":22,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"22It takes half an hour for a cow-manure fire to reach 950 degrees.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"FALSE","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q23","ordinal":23,"type":"true_false_not_given","prompt":"23E-coli is the most difficult bacterium to remove from water by filtration.","options":[{"label":"TRUE","text":"TRUE"},{"label":"FALSE","text":"FALSE"},{"label":"NOT GIVEN","text":"NOT GIVEN"}],"correct_answer":"NOT GIVEN","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s2:g3","ordinal":3,"instructions":"Questions 24-26 Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D. Write the correct letter in boxes 24–26 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q24","ordinal":24,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"24The Manatuto project aimed to set up a","options":[{"label":"A","text":"charitable trust"},{"label":"B","text":"filtration experiment"},{"label":"C","text":"water filter factory"},{"label":"D","text":"community kiln"}],"correct_answer":"C","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q25","ordinal":25,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"25To be effective, the Flynn filters must","options":[{"label":"A","text":"remove all dangerous pathogens"},{"label":"B","text":"be a particular thickness"},{"label":"C","text":"filter water as quickly as possible"},{"label":"D","text":"be made from 100 per cent clay"}],"correct_answer":"B","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q26","ordinal":26,"type":"multiple_choice","prompt":"26Flynn does not intend to patent his filter because he","options":[{"label":"A","text":"wants it be freely available"},{"label":"B","text":"has produced a very simple design"},{"label":"C","text":"cannot make a profit in poor countries"},{"label":"D","text":"has already given the idea to a charity"}],"correct_answer":"A","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s3","kind":"reading","ordinal":3,"duration_seconds":1200,"stimulus":{"type":"passage","title":"Putting the Brakes on Climate Change","paragraphs":[{"label":null,"text":"Are hydrogen cars the answer?"},{"label":"A","text":"It is tempting to think that the conservation of coral reefs and rainforests is a separate issue from traffic and air pollution. But it is not. Scientists are now confident that rapid changes in the Earth’s climate are already disrupting and altering many wildlife habitats. Pollution from vehicles is a big part of the problem."},{"label":"B","text":"The United Nation’s Climate Change Panel has estimated that the global average temperature rise expected by the year 2100 could be as much as 6°C, causing forest fires and dieback on land and coral bleaching in the ocean. Few species, if any, will be immune from the changes in temperature, rainfall and sea levels. The panel believes that if such catastrophic temperature rises are to be avoided, the quantity of greenhouse gases, especially carbon dioxide, being released into the atmosphere must be reduced. That will depend on slowing the rate of deforestation and, more crucially, finding alternatives to coal, oil and gas as our principal energy sources."},{"label":"C","text":"Technologies do exist to reduce or eliminate carbon dioxide as a waste product of our energy consumption. Wind power and solar power are both spreading fast, but what are we doing about traffic? Electric cars are one possible option, but their range and the time it takes to charge their batteries pose serious limitations. However, the technology that shows the most potential to make cars climate-friendly is fuel-cell technology. This was actually invented in the late nineteenth century, but because the world’s motor industry put its effort into developing the combustion engine, it was never refined for mass production. One of the first prototype fuel-cell-powered vehicles has been built by the Ford Motor Company. It is like a conventional car, only with better acceleration and a smoother ride. Ford engineers expect to be able to produce a virtually silent vehicle in the future."},{"label":"D","text":"So what’s the process involved – and is there a catch? Hydrogen goes into the fuel tank, producing electricity. The only emission from the exhaust pipe is water. The fuel-cell is, in some ways, similar to a battery, but unlike a battery it does not run down. As long as hydrogen and oxygen are supplied to the cell, it will keep on generating electricity. Some cells work off methane and a few use liquid fuels such as methanol, but fuel-cells using hydrogen probably have the most potential. Furthermore, they need not be limited to transport. Fuel-cells can be made in a huge range of size, small enough for portable computers or large enough for power stations. They have no moving parts and therefore need no oil. They just need a supply of hydrogen. The big question, then, is where to get it from."},{"label":"E","text":"One source of hydrogen is water. But to exploit the abundant resource, electricity is needed, and if the electricity is produced by a coal-fired power station or other fossil fuel, then the overall carbon reduction benefit of the fuel-cell disappears. Renewable sources, such as wind and solar power, do not produce enough energy for it to be economically viable to use them in the ‘manufacture’ of hydrogen as a transport fuel. Another source of hydrogen is, however, available and could provide a supply pending the development of more efficient and cheaper renewable energy technologies. By splitting natural gas (methane) into its constituent parts, hydrogen and carbon dioxide are produced. One way round the problem of what to do with the carbon dioxide could be to store it back below ground – so called geological sequestration. Oil companies, such as Norway’s Statoil, are experimenting with storing carbon dioxide below ground in oil and gas wells."},{"label":"F","text":"With freak weather conditions, arguably caused by global warming, frequently in the headlines, the urgent need to get fuel-cell vehicles will be available in most showrooms. Even now, fuel-cell buses are operating in the US, while in Germany a courier company is planning to take delivery of fuel-cell-powered vans in the near future. The fact that centrally-run fleets of buses and vans are the first fuel-cell vehicles identifies another challenge – fuel distribution. The refueling facilities necessary to top up hydrogen-powered vehicles are available only in a very few places at present. Public transport and delivery firms are logical places to start, since their vehicles are operated from central depots."},{"label":"G","text":"Fuel-cell technology is being developed right across the automotive industry. This technology could have a major impact in slowing down climate change, but further investment is needed if the industry – and the world’s wildlife – is to have a long-term future."}]},"groups":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s3:g1","ordinal":1,"instructions":"Questions 27-32 Reading Passage 3 has seven paragraphs, A–G. Choose the correct heading for paragraphs A–F from the list of headings below. Select the correct number, i–ix, using the buttons below.","shared_options":[{"label":"i","text":"Action already taken by the United Nations"},{"label":"ii","text":"Marketing the hydrogen car"},{"label":"iii","text":"Making the new technology available worldwide"},{"label":"iv","text":"Some negative predictions from one group of experts"},{"label":"v","text":"How the new vehicle technology works"},{"label":"vi","text":"The history of fuel-cell technology"},{"label":"vii","text":"A holistic view of climate change"},{"label":"viii","text":"Locating the essential ingredient"},{"label":"ix","text":"Sustaining car manufacture"}],"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q27","ordinal":27,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"27 Paragraph A ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"vii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q28","ordinal":28,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"28 Paragraph B ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"iv","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q29","ordinal":29,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"29 Paragraph C ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"vi","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q30","ordinal":30,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"30 Paragraph D ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"v","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q31","ordinal":31,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"31 Paragraph E ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"viii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q32","ordinal":32,"type":"matching_headings","prompt":"32 Paragraph F ____","options":[{"label":"i","text":"i"},{"label":"ii","text":"ii"},{"label":"iii","text":"iii"},{"label":"iv","text":"iv"},{"label":"v","text":"v"},{"label":"vi","text":"vi"},{"label":"vii","text":"vii"},{"label":"viii","text":"viii"},{"label":"ix","text":"ix"}],"correct_answer":"iii","accepted_variants":[],"evidence":null}]},{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:s3:g2","ordinal":2,"instructions":"Questions 33-36 Complete the sentences below. Choose NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer. Write your answers in boxes 33–36 below.","shared_options":null,"questions":[{"external_id":"full-cd-ielts-reading-practice-test-3-3:q33","ordinal":33,"type":"sentence_completion","prompt":"33 In the late nineteenth century, the car i